Fixed. The cause was in `Semaphore::forget_permits` (`tokio/src/sync/batch_semaphore.rs:376`):

```rust
let curr = curr_bits >> Self::PERMIT_SHIFT;
let new = curr.saturating_sub(n);
self.permits.compare_exchange_weak(curr_bits, new << Self::PERMIT_SHIFT, ...)
```

The permit counter packs the closed flag into bit 0 (`CLOSED = 1`, `PERMIT_SHIFT = 1`). The CAS rebuilt the whole word from the shifted permit count alone, so the closed bit was silently dropped — `close()` followed by `forget_permits(n)` left `is_closed()` false and `acquire` handing out permits again. The fix ORs the flag back in:

```rust
// Preserve the closed flag; rebuilding the counter from the
// permit count alone would reopen a closed semaphore.
let new_bits = (new << Self::PERMIT_SHIFT) | (curr_bits & Self::CLOSED);
match self.permits.compare_exchange_weak(curr_bits, new_bits, AcqRel, Acquire) {
```

Reading `curr_bits` (rather than a separate load) inside the CAS loop keeps this correct against a concurrent `close()`: if `close()` sets the bit between the load and the exchange, the CAS fails and retries against the updated word.

Tests: `sync_semaphore` 18/18 pass — `forget_closed` was the failing one and now passes; `--lib` (137), `sync_semaphore_owned`, `sync_mutex`, and `sync_rwlock` all pass. Note `cargo fmt` isn't installed in this environment, so I matched formatting by hand; the one over-100-column line in the file (line 230) is pre-existing.
